#limitation(title: [NMES Safety Not Established in ME/CFS — NOT a Clinical Recommendation])[
NMES is presented here as a *hypothetical* countermeasure only. No ME/CFS-specific NMES safety or efficacy trial exists. The critical unanswered question is whether electrically-induced muscle contractions trigger PEM: if the metabolic cost of muscle activation is the PEM trigger, NMES contractions — which are metabolically indistinguishable from voluntary contractions — could trigger PEM identically to volitional exercise. This is not a merely theoretical concern. First, electrical stimulation provokes the same muscle micro-trauma as exercise: it causes histological fibre and connective-tissue alteration, circulating creatine kinase elevation, and delayed-onset muscle soreness, with damage magnitude from electrically-evoked contractions comparable to maximal voluntary *eccentric* contractions — driven by non-selective, synchronous, spatially-fixed motor-unit recruitment @Nosaka2011ESmuscleDamage; a single unaccustomed NMES bout (100 quadriceps + 100 hamstrings contractions) raised serum creatine kinase to roughly 2300 IU/L @Vanderthommen2015NMESrepeatedBout. This is the same damage-then-inflammatory cascade that voluntary exercise induces, so a PEM trigger via the damage pathway is mechanistically plausible, not merely speculative. Second, hybrid electrical stimulation raises oxygen uptake and heart rate comparably to voluntary effort @Tajima2021hybridNMESoxygen, and electrical pulse stimulation of ME/CFS muscle fails to activate AMPK and glucose uptake @Brown2015EPSAMPK — a contraction-metabolic defect consistent with a PEM-prone response. A rare rhabdomyolysis case in electrical muscle stimulation documents a real, if uncommon, harm @vonDeneen2024EMSrhabdo. The counterweight is that the 1990s studies found normal electrically-stimulated force in ME/CFS @Gibson1993CFSmuscle @Edwards1993muscleHistopathology @Jamal1991postviralNeurophysiology — consistent with a central (not peripheral) limitation, in which case EMS might bypass the deficit and be relatively benign; the tension between this legacy null and the modern membrane/metabolic defects is unresolved and is exactly why EMS safety cannot be assumed in either direction without a trial. A further nuance is that muscle damage from NMES is intensity-dependent: low-intensity NMES (approximately 15% of maximal tetanic force) drives hypertrophy without overt damage @Fessard2025NMESnoDamage, so any safety margin depends on keeping intensity far below the damaging range — which the minimal protocol below attempts, but which has never been validated in ME/CFS. A further objection is timescale: exercise-induced muscle damage and DOMS resolve over days, whereas PEM persists for days-to-weeks, so either the damage pathway is not the whole PEM story (a central/metabolic component also operates), or micro-tears initiate a downstream cascade that outlasts the damage itself — the former remains more parsimonious and is why the damage-PEM link stays an inference, not a demonstration. Ch17 @oq:bedbound-nmes articulates this: whether even 5 minutes at the lowest visible contraction intensity in a single muscle group is tolerated is completely unknown. The protocol below reflects ICU rehabilitation parameters @Dirks2015NMES (n=6, within-subject crossover, 7 days, fully sedated patients); ME/CFS-appropriate parameters, if any exist, would need to be far lower. Contraindicated in patients with pacemakers or implanted electrical devices.
]
